Good catch, and it is worse than just `siteDetails`: `?category=`, `?group=` and `?storageAccessState=` all had it, and an empty `?title=` was blanking the toolbar on a page that was otherwise fine.

I fixed it one level up from where you suggested, in `parseUrlArguments`, by not putting an empty value into the bundle at all. I think that might be a better catch-all solution:

```java
String val = uri.getQueryParameter(param);

//"?site=" and "?site" both parse to an empty value, which names nothing. Treating
// it as absent is what every reader of these arguments already expects, and it
// keeps the required argument check in resolve() a question of presence alone.
if (val == null || val.isEmpty()) continue;
```

Three reasons for putting the check a level above rather than checking for it in the `requireArgs` loop:

1. It covers the arguments that are not required too, like the empty `?title=` above, which the requireArgs loop never sees.
2. The `instanceof String` version needs `Bundle.get(String)`, the only accessor that reads a
value without knowing its type.
3. `resolve()` stays a plain presence check instead of growing a type switch.

Tests for it are in `testResolveRedirectsWhenRequiredArgumentIsEmpty` (covering `?site=`, `?site`, `?group=` and `?category=`) and `testParseUrlArgumentsSkipsEmptyValues`.

