Yes, these are great points, and they expose some real issues imo.

To fix this, I think we need some additional validation logic. I refactored `createFromPreferenceKey` inside of `SiteSettingsCategory` so that we could check if an arbitrary preference key maps to a real key. E.g., factored out the logic in `createFromPreferenceKey` into `typeFromPreferenceKey`

```java
  private static @Type int typeFromPreferenceKey(@Nullable String preferenceKey) {
      assert Type.ALL_SITES == 0;
      for (@Type int i = Type.ALL_SITES; i < Type.NUM_ENTRIES; i++) {
          if (preferenceKey(i).equals(preferenceKey)) return i;
      }
      return Type.NUM_ENTRIES;
  }
```

Then, use that to determine whether the arbitrary supplied preference key is valid:
```
  public static boolean isValidPreferenceKey(@Nullable String preferenceKey) {
    return typeFromPreferenceKey(preferenceKey) != Type.NUM_ENTRIES;
  }
```

And then updated the definition for `createFromPreferenceKey` to use `typeFromPreferenceKey`:
```
  public static @Nullable SiteSettingsCategory createFromPreferenceKey(
          BrowserContextHandle browserContextHandle, String preferenceKey) {
      @Type int type = typeFromPreferenceKey(preferenceKey);
      return type == Type.NUM_ENTRIES ? null : createFromType(browserContextHandle, type);
  }
```

The two routes then treat an unknown category differently, on purpose:

- `/siteSettings/category` redirects to `/siteSettings`. There is no page for a category that does not

